import express from "express";

const app = express();

app.post("/echo/:roomId", express.json(), (req, res) => {
  res.json({
    params: req.params,
    query: req.query,
    contentType: req.get("content-type"),
    body: req.body,
  });
});

app.listen(Number(process.env.PORT ?? 4000), "127.0.0.1");

steps.body:
A list endpoint that returns everything works with three rooms and breaks with three thousand: the answer gets slow, big, and useless on a phone. Query parameters let a client ask for just what it needs, a filtered, sorted page of the list, and they're where most of the reading-text-into-values work happens.

## Roombook's room filters

`GET /v1/rooms` takes these parameters:

- `building=main-library`: only rooms in that building, named by its **slug**, a short name made for URLs.
- `minCapacity=4`: only rooms that fit at least 4 people.
- `amenity=whiteboard`: only rooms that have it. Repeat it to ask for several, `amenity=whiteboard&amenity=screen`, and a room must have all of them.
- `sort=capacity`: the order, `name` or `capacity`, with `name` as the default.
- `limit=20` and `cursor=...`: the page size and where to start, explained below.

## Reading them in Express

Express 5 parses the query string with its **simple** parser: each name becomes a string, and a name that appears more than once becomes an array of strings. Express 4 used a parser that also built nested objects out of names like `room[capacity]`, which surprised people and caused security bugs, so Express 5 turned it off by default. `req.query` is also read-only now: you can't write the parsed values back into it, so they go into a new object.

`src/modules/rooms/rooms.query.ts` turns `req.query` into that object, a typed `RoomQuery`, or into a message saying what's wrong:

```ts
const NAMES = ["building", "minCapacity", "amenity", "sort", "limit", "cursor"];
const SORTS = ["name", "capacity"];

export function parseRoomQuery(query: Record<string, unknown>): RoomQuery | string {
  const unknown = Object.keys(query).find((name) => !NAMES.includes(name));
  if (unknown !== undefined) return `${unknown} is not a known parameter`;

  const text = (name: string) => {
    const value = query[name];
    return typeof value === "string" ? value : undefined;
  };

  const minText = text("minCapacity");
  const minCapacity = minText === undefined ? undefined : Number(minText);
  if (minCapacity !== undefined && !Number.isInteger(minCapacity)) return "minCapacity must be a whole number";

  const sort = text("sort") ?? "name";
  if (!SORTS.includes(sort)) return `sort must be one of: ${SORTS.join(", ")}`;

  const limit = Number(text("limit") ?? 20);
  if (!Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 1 || limit > 100) return "limit must be a whole number from 1 to 100";

  const cursorText = text("cursor");
  const cursor = cursorText === undefined ? undefined : decodeCursor(cursorText);
  if (cursorText !== undefined && !cursor) return "cursor is not one this API gave out";

  const amenity = query.amenity ?? [];
  const amenities = (Array.isArray(amenity) ? amenity : [amenity]).map(String);

  return { building: text("building"), minCapacity, amenities, sort, limit, cursor };
}
```

The route answers a message with **422**, since the request was readable but a value broke a rule, in the same first-draft shape as the routes lesson's 404s: `res.status(422).json({ status: 422, title: query })`. The lesson on validation and errors replaces this hand-written checking with a schema, and the error with one that lists every field that's wrong.

Four habits are in that code:

- **Defaults.** A missing `sort` means `name`, and a missing `limit` means 20. A client should get a sensible answer from the bare URL.
- **Bounds.** `limit` can't go over 100, or one request could ask for a million rooms and tie up the server building the answer.
- **An allowlist for sorting.** Only names on the list are accepted. In the raw SQL lesson, the sort becomes part of a query in SQL, the language databases speak, and taking a column name from a client as it is would let them sort by anything, like a password column, or slip in commands of their own.
- **Unknown parameters are refused.** `?colour=blue` gets a 422 titled `colour is not a known parameter`. Ignoring it would look friendlier, but then a typo like `minCapcity=6` would quietly return every room, and the client would never learn why its filter didn't work.

## Pages: offset or cursor

The obvious way to page through a list is an **offset**: `?offset=20&limit=10` means skip 20, then take 10. It's simple, and it breaks when the list changes between two requests, as the top half of the diagram shows:

```diagram request-data-pagination
With an offset, a new item at the front pushes C onto the next page too. A cursor remembers C itself.
```

A **cursor** says where the last page ended instead: start after C. New items arriving earlier in the list can't shift it, and the database can jump straight to the spot, while an offset of 100,000 makes it count through 100,000 rows first.

Roombook's list answers carry a `nextCursor` next to `items`. The client sends it back as `cursor` to get the next page, and a `null` means there are no more:

```ts
export function encodeCursor(cursor: Cursor): string {
  return Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(cursor)).toString("base64url");
}
```

The cursor holds the last item's sort value and its id, since two rooms can share a capacity, written as JSON and then in **base64url**, a way to write any bytes with only letters, digits, `-`, and `_`, which are safe in a URL. Clients treat it as an opaque token: they pass it back unchanged and never build one themselves, which leaves Roombook free to change what's inside.

The body is the only place a client can send real content, and the only place where the client controls how much arrives. A careless API reads whatever comes, however big, in whatever format, and that's how one request runs a server out of memory, or how a form post gets silently ignored. Roombook's rules for bodies are short and strict.

## JSON only, or 415

Roombook's bodies are JSON. The line in `app.ts` is `app.use(express.json({ limit: "100kb", type: ["application/json", "application/merge-patch+json"] }))`. It reads a body only when its `Content-Type` is one of those two types, and otherwise leaves `req.body` as `undefined`. A handler that then reads `req.body.name` crashes with a TypeError, which Express answers with a 500, an error that blames the server for the client's mistake.

The right answer is **415 Unsupported Media Type**: I can't read that format. Roombook checks before the handler runs, with a small function in `src/http/require-json.ts`:

```ts
export const requireJson: RequestHandler = (req, res, next) => {
  if (!req.is(["application/json", "application/merge-patch+json"])) {
    res.status(415).json({ status: 415, title: "Body must be JSON" });
    return;
  }
  next();
};
```

`req.is` checks the request's `Content-Type` against a list. If it matches, `next()` passes the request on to the handler. Every route that takes a body lists it first, as in `.post(requireJson, (req, res) => ...)`. A function that runs before a handler like this is middleware, which the next lesson covers.

## A size limit, or 413

`express.json({ limit: "100kb", ... })` stops reading a body after 100 kilobytes and answers **413 Content Too Large**. A booking is about 200 bytes, so 100 KB is far more than any real request needs. Without a limit, a client could send a gigabyte, and the server would try to hold it all in memory, then block the event loop parsing it. 100 KB is also the default, but writing it down makes the choice visible.

If the body is JSON but broken, like a missing bracket, `express.json()` answers **400 Bad Request**. Both 413 and 400 arrive as small HTML pages for now, and the lesson on validation and errors turns them into Roombook's JSON errors.

## Forms and file uploads

APIs mostly take JSON, but you'll meet two other formats:

- `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` is what an HTML form sends by default, `name=Hamilton&capacity=10`. `express.urlencoded()` reads it.
- `multipart/form-data` carries files, like a photo of a room, in parts. Express can't read it by itself, and the usual package is **multer**.

A big upload shouldn't sit in memory. `req` is a **stream**, data that arrives in chunks, so the server can pass each chunk on to storage as it comes, and memory stays small whatever the file's size.

## No body on GET or DELETE

Recall from the method table in the routes lesson: a body on GET or DELETE has no agreed meaning, and many proxies drop it. So Roombook never reads one, and a client that needs to send data to a read uses the query string.

## Missing or null, in a PATCH

The routes lesson left one question open: how does a handler tell a field that's missing from one that's `null`? A booking's note shows why it matters. `{"attendees": 4}` should keep the note, and `{"note": null}` should clear it. `??` can't tell them apart, so `PATCH /v1/bookings/:bookingId` asks whether the field is in the body at all:

```ts sketch
const patch = req.body;
const updated = bookings.update({
  ...booking,
  attendees: patch.attendees ?? booking.attendees,
  note: Object.hasOwn(patch, "note") ? patch.note : booking.note,
});
```

`Object.hasOwn(patch, "note")` is true when the client sent a `note` field, even one set to `null`, and false when they left it out. Use it for every field that can be cleared.
